**Basic stack.** Nodes hold `value` and `next`. `push` makes a node whose `next` is the old head; `pop` returns the head's value and moves head to `next`, or signals empty.

**Thread-safe with a lock.** Wrap `push` and `pop` in the same mutex (`synchronized`, `ReentrantLock`, or `threading.Lock` in Python). Both read and write `head`, so both need the lock. It is simple and obviously correct, but threads block, and a thread holding the lock that is descheduled stalls everyone.

**Lock-free with CAS (Treiber stack).** CAS(addr, expected, new) atomically sets addr to new only if it still equals expected, and reports success.

```java
class LockFreeStack<T> {
  private static class Node<T> { final T v; Node<T> next; Node(T v){this.v=v;} }
  private final AtomicReference<Node<T>> head = new AtomicReference<>();

  void push(T v) {
    Node<T> n = new Node<>(v);
    do { n.next = head.get(); }
    while (!head.compareAndSet(n.next, n));
  }
  T pop() {
    Node<T> h;
    do {
      h = head.get();
      if (h == null) return null;      // empty
    } while (!head.compareAndSet(h, h.next));
    return h.v;
  }
}
```

If another thread changed `head` between the read and the CAS, the CAS fails and the loop retries with fresh state. Some thread always makes progress (lock-free), though an individual thread may retry repeatedly.

**ABA problem.** `head` is A; thread 1 reads A and its `next` B; thread 2 pops A, pops B, pushes A back; thread 1's CAS succeeds because head is still A, but `B` is no longer in the stack. In garbage-collected languages, fresh node objects mostly avoid this. In C/C++ with reused memory, use tagged pointers (a version counter), hazard pointers, or epoch reclamation. Java also has `AtomicStampedReference`.

**Python note:** it has no user-level CAS on plain objects; with the GIL you would simulate it with a lock or use `queue.LifoQueue`. Say so, and write the CAS version in Java or C++.
